--TEST--
Under `[regex] backtracking = "deny"`, a pattern built at run time that only the backtracking engine accepts throws a catchable `RuntimeError` at the call that compiles it, and a malformed one keeps its own message — `rule:core-classes/regex-compile-time-tiering`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// No pattern here is a `Core\Regex::compile` literal, so the program compiles.
// Each pattern is compiled when the call runs.
class Patterns {
    public static function behind(string $look): string {
        return '(?<=' . $look . ')X';
    }
}

try {
    Core\Regex::compile(Patterns::behind('a'));
    echo "compiled\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// A member that takes the pattern directly throws the same way. The second
// call gives the same error as the first.
for (var $i = 0; $i < 2; $i++) {
    try {
        echo Core\Regex::matches('aX', Patterns::behind('a')) ? "matched\n" : "no\n";
    } catch (RuntimeError $e) {
        echo $e->message, "\n";
    }
}

// A malformed pattern still gives the error for a malformed pattern.
try {
    Core\Regex::compile(Patterns::behind('('));
    echo "compiled\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $e) {
    echo Core\Str::contains($e->message, 'not a pattern either engine can compile') ? "malformed\n" : $e->message . "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[regex]
backtracking = "deny"
--EXPECT--
Core\Regex::compile(): `(?<=a)X` needs the backtracking regex engine. `[regex] backtracking` is `deny` in `nvs.toml`. Write the pattern without lookaround and without backreferences.
Core\Regex::matches(): `(?<=a)X` needs the backtracking regex engine. `[regex] backtracking` is `deny` in `nvs.toml`. Write the pattern without lookaround and without backreferences.
Core\Regex::matches(): `(?<=a)X` needs the backtracking regex engine. `[regex] backtracking` is `deny` in `nvs.toml`. Write the pattern without lookaround and without backreferences.
malformed
